text:
alex.ng: Hey infra folks — quick sanity check. I'm prototyping per-namespace KMS keys for EKS so each namespace has its own key and only pods in that namespace can decrypt. Using IRSA for pod-to-role mapping. Question: can we enforce namespace-level restriction in the KMS key policy by matching principal tags/session tags from the IRSA role, or do we need one IAM role per namespace and reference the role ARN directly in the key policy?

maria.chen: short answer: safer to use 1 role per namespace if you want key-policy enforcement. But paste your TF so we can look. :+1:

alex.ng: pasted a reduced TF snippet (trying to keep minimal):
```hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "eks_sa_role" {
  name = "eks-sa-role-${var.namespace}"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Effect = "Allow"
      Principal = {
        Federated = aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.eks.arn
      }
      Action = "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"
      Condition = {
        StringEquals = {"sts:aud" = "sts.amazonaws.com"}
      }
    }]
  })
  tags = { "k8s-namespace" = var.namespace }
}

resource "aws_kms_key" "ns_key" {
  description = "KMS key for namespace ${var.namespace}"
  policy = <<POLICY
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Id": "key-default-1",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "Allow administration",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::${data.aws_caller_identity.current.account_id}:root"},
      "Action": "kms:*",
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "Allow decrypt for ns role",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::${data.aws_caller_identity.current.account_id}:role/eks-sa-role-${var.namespace}"},
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt","kms:Encrypt","kms:ReEncrypt*","kms:GenerateDataKey*"],
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "Attempt using principal tag (experimental)",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "*"},
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt"],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/k8s-namespace": "${var.namespace}"}
      },
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
POLICY
}
```

alex.ng: My worry: tags on the role here are static, but when IRSA is used pods assume the role via web identity — do session tags get propagated such that aws:PrincipalTag check works? Or does the role need to be uniquely created per namespace?

jamal: Good question. IRSA uses sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity. Session tags can be passed when assuming if you call STS with TagSpecifications (tag session) but k8s serviceaccount projected token flow does NOT automatically add session tags. So you can't rely on role having dynamic session tags from the pod.

jamal: Practically: two options — (A) create a dedicated role per namespace and reference its ARN in the key policy (simpler to reason about), or (B) create a single role but use kms:EncryptionContext or kms:CallerAccount combined with an application-level envelope key to enforce namespace boundaries.

maria.chen: +1. Also note AWS KMS key policies treat Principal ARNs literally. Using aws:PrincipalTag requires the principal to actually have that tag in the call context. IRSA won't add principal tags unless you explicitly pass them when calling STS, which k8s runtime doesn't.

alex.ng: OK — leaning to per-namespace role. That means more roles to manage but simpler key policy. Any best-practice for automating role creation in TF?

maria.chen: We have a small module that parameterizes the role name and attaches minimal kms permissions. Reuse that. Also add lifecycle prevent_destroy on keys and roles to avoid accidental deletes during namespace teardown.

jamal: If you want a minimal key policy snippet for per-role approach, here's one:
```json
{
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect":"Allow",
    "Principal":{"AWS":"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/eks-sa-role-staging"},
    "Action":["kms:Decrypt","kms:Encrypt","kms:GenerateDataKey*"],
    "Resource":"*"
  }]
}
```

